# Active Work Queue

This file tracks the next concrete work. The broader sequence and exit criteria live in `docs/modernization-roadmap.md`.

Rules for completing an item:

- Work on one logical change branch at a time.
- Understand and document the current behavior before changing it.
- Record material architecture decisions as ADRs.
- Add meaningful automated tests and verify the relevant build.
- Update documentation in the same work item.
- Do not mark an item complete when only the happy path works.

## 1. Credential and security cleanup — next

- [ ] Identify secrets, credentials, personal phone numbers, and unsafe local configuration in source, tests, and configuration files.
- [ ] Revoke and rotate every exposed Razorpay, Twilio, JWT, database, or provider credential; treat committed values as compromised.
- [ ] Remove sensitive values from the current tree and Git history using a reviewed, coordinated procedure.
- [ ] Replace secrets with environment-variable configuration and commit only safe examples.
- [ ] Ensure services fail clearly and safely when required configuration is absent.
- [ ] Add secret scanning and repository ignore rules.
- [ ] Review logs and API responses for tokens, OTPs, payment data, provider secrets, phone numbers, and other personal data.
- [ ] Document local secret setup without including real values.
- [ ] Verify the cleaned repository with automated secret scanning.

Important: deleting a secret from the latest commit does not make it safe. Rotation is required before history cleanup.

## 2. Reproducible development baseline

- [ ] Capture and document the clean baseline build.
- [ ] Add Maven Wrapper support.
- [ ] Provide containerized local dependencies and health checks.
- [ ] Add safe example configuration for every service.
- [ ] Fix and document gRPC addresses and ports.
- [ ] Document startup order, shutdown, and smoke-test commands.

## 3. Service-by-service modernization

Review one service at a time. Choose the order after the security baseline and local build are understood.

For every service:

- [ ] State its business purpose, responsibilities, and explicit non-goals.
- [ ] Identify its source of truth and enforce database ownership.
- [ ] Inventory REST, gRPC, and Kafka contracts.
- [ ] Document synchronous dependencies, timeouts, and failure behavior.
- [ ] Document events with owner, purpose, stable name, event ID, version, and timestamp.
- [ ] Move business rules out of controllers and validate inputs at the boundary.
- [ ] Enforce authentication, authorization, roles, and resource ownership as appropriate.
- [ ] Define consistent errors, logs, health checks, and operational behavior.
- [ ] Add unit tests for business rules and meaningful failure paths.
- [ ] Add integration or contract tests using disposable or fake dependencies.
- [ ] Update module-level setup, API, and architecture documentation.
- [ ] Verify the module and relevant reactor build before selecting the next service.

Services to review:

- [ ] `user-service`
- [ ] `product-service`
- [ ] `category-service`
- [ ] `cart-service`
- [ ] `order-service`
- [ ] `filter-service`
- [ ] `api-gateway`
- [ ] `service-registry`
- [ ] `cloud-config-server`
- [ ] `proto`

## 4. Complete the reference journey

- [ ] Authenticate a customer safely.
- [ ] Browse authoritative product information.
- [ ] Modify only the authenticated customer's cart.
- [ ] Validate products, prices, availability, and ownership during checkout.
- [ ] Use `BigDecimal` with explicit currency and rounding rules.
- [ ] Define inventory reservation and release behavior.
- [ ] Create idempotent orders with explicit state transitions.
- [ ] Complete payment initiation, verification, callbacks, and failure handling through a provider abstraction.
- [ ] Publish versioned order events reliably.
- [ ] Deliver notifications asynchronously through fake/local providers in tests.

## 5. Quality, operations, and portfolio readiness

- [ ] Add reliable event publication, consumer idempotency, retries, and dead-letter handling.
- [ ] Add schema migrations, structured logs, correlation IDs, metrics, and traces.
- [ ] Define resiliency policies and graceful degradation.
- [ ] Add CI coverage for the clean build and reference journey.
- [ ] Verify and publish sanitized API examples and demo data.
- [ ] Add sequence diagrams for the completed business flows.
- [ ] Document scale assumptions, bottlenecks, consistency choices, and alternative designs.
- [ ] Ensure every resume or LinkedIn claim is supported by running code, tests, or clearly labeled future work.
